Author an **arcane spell nova**, a burst of magic cast once, as a 3D particle
**system** using only the particle tool.

It is the flash of energy that erupts when a spell detonates at a point: a bright
white flash at the center, a thin ring of energy sweeping outward across the ground,
a fountain of glowing rune-spark motes launched upward and arcing back down, and a
soft glow that blooms and fades. It is a **one-shot** effect — **one cast**: the game
plays a fresh instance each time the spell is cast, so you are authoring the *effect*
(a single cast, which bursts and decays to empty), not a single frame of it.

## How the particle tool works

The only way to shape the effect is the `particle-3d` binary already on your
`PATH`. It is the sole channel, and — this is the key idea — you do **not** place
individual particles. You **author a system**: emitters that spawn particles,
forces that push them around, and per-particle curves for size, opacity, and color
over each particle's life. The review UI and the game **simulate that system live**,
exactly the way a real particle editor (Unreal's Niagara, Unity's VFX Graph) plays
a system. Because it is a live stochastic simulation, the effect **varies slightly
from one play to the next** — so build something that reads as an arcane nova across
replays and from any orbit angle, not a frozen arrangement.

You build the system by calling `particle-3d` **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered list of operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — is the
**authoritative output that is scored**. A system produced any other way is
discarded and flagged; only the recorded operations count.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
particle-3d add-emitter --name flash --shape point --burst 8 --at 0 --lifetime 90 --speed 4
```

This effect is a **one-shot**: keep the timeline one-shot so it bursts at the start
and decays cleanly to empty, and emit as timed **bursts** at the cast instant, not
as a continuous rate. Rendering is a separate, on-request step: run `particle-3d render` to
**simulate the system over its duration, write the preview animation, and emit the
`system.json` your result is built from**. An unrendered effect leaves an empty
system, so you **must** run `render` before you finish. The field dimensions,
duration, and playback fps are already seeded in a config next to your workspace,
so no operation needs those flags.

Run `particle-3d --help` to list every operation and `particle-3d <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read the rendered `effect.gif` after your calls** to judge the
effect's read, motion, and decay, and decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Author the nova system operation by operation — the central flash, the outward
ground ring, the upward rune-spark fountain, and the soft glow (as timed bursts at
the cast instant), with the forces and per-particle curves that sweep the ring out,
arc the sparks up and back under gravity, and fade each layer as the brief
describes, and the timeline kept **one-shot** — running `particle-3d render` to
simulate and check the effect as you go. When the simulated `effect.gif` reads as a
single arcane nova that decays cleanly to empty, run `render` one last time and
stop: the recorded `actions.json` (and the `system.json` it emits) is your finished
submission.
